{
  "requirement": "After one valid judgment, the harness MUST write the subject text, submitted\ndetermination and rationale, and recorded inputs using the existing evidence\nschema. {cite}`requirements.ci.schema`\nBy default, it MUST write the subject's evidence file. With `--stdout`, it MUST\nemit a single evidence JSON object followed by a newline to stdout and MUST NOT\ncreate or change evidence files or directories. All three outcomes MUST be\nwritten to the selected destination as completed assessments; their validation\nstatus MUST follow {cite}`requirements.ci.determination`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the sources satisfy `requirements.authoring.persistence`.\n\n**Implementation**\n\n- **Record content and schema.** `record` (src/authoring/mod.rs:787) builds the evidence object after one valid judgment. It contains the definition text keyed by `requirement` or `example`, the determination, the rationale, the `references` digest and the recorded `inputs`. This matches `requirements.ci.schema`.\n- **Citation reviews.** A citation review uses its own shape. That belongs to `requirements.references.citation_review`, which is out of scope here.\n- **Three outcomes.** `Judgment::to_value` (src/judgment.rs:43) serializes `true`, `false` and `{\"FIXME\":[…]}`. `assess` (src/authoring/mod.rs:804) returns the record only after `submission` has validated exactly one judgment with a nonempty rationale.\n- **Validation status.** Persistence writes false judgments like the others. Whether they pass validation is decided by `requirements.ci.determination`. That is outside this requirement's code, and its text matches. The test `false_is_a_completed_assessment` confirms that a false record is written as a completed assessment and then fails validation.\n- **Default destination.**\n  - `configured_assessment` (mod.rs:378) writes pretty JSON plus a newline to `AuthoringSubject::evidence_path`, which is `evidence/<name>.json`. It creates the directory if needed.\n  - The batch path does the same through `write_record` (batch.rs:287) and `persisted`. It replaces the file atomically through a temp file, which `requirements.authoring.batch` also marks as implemented.\n- **`--stdout`.**\n  - `configured_assessment` writes one JSON object plus a newline to stdout. It does not touch the project's `evidence/` files or directories.\n  - `--all` conflicts with `--stdout` in clap, so the batch path never writes evidence to stdout.\n- **Failed writes.** `evidence_written` is set only after a successful write or emit. A failed write fails the run and leaves the usage log with `evidence_written` false.\n- **Marking.** Every place that implements part of the requirement carries an `{impl}` marker: `evidence_path`, `persisted`, `write_record`, `record`, `assess`, `to_value` and `configured_assessment`. The only `{cite}` in source is on the `PendingAssessment` struct (mod.rs:118). It is a pure data holder with no persistence logic, so I do not count it as an unmarked implementation. I found no behavior in the `{impl}` code that this requirement or another definition does not specify.\n\n**Tests**\n\n- **`stdout_emits_only_evidence_json_without_changing_project_files`** (tests/authoring_cli.rs:1075) covers `--stdout` for true, false and FIXME, with and without verbose.\n  - It parses stdout as exactly one JSON object ending in a newline.\n  - It checks the `requirement`, `determination`, `rationale` and `inputs` fields, including outline, definition and requirements inputs.\n  - It confirms the existing evidence file is byte-identical.\n  - It confirms the `evidence/` directory is not created when it was absent.\n- **Default file destination.**\n  - `false_is_a_completed_assessment` checks the evidence file for a false judgment. The rationale is persisted, outline inputs hold only selectors and hashes, and validation rejects the record.\n  - `ollama_cli_delivers_sources_records_fixme…` checks the FIXME shape and that validation accepts it.\n  - `verbose_single_author_reports_only_false_rationale…` checks the persisted determination and rationale for true and false.\n  - The batch tests in tests/authoring_batch.rs check persisted determinations for true, false and FIXME. They also check that denied reads write no evidence for the failed subject while the other subjects' evidence is kept.\n  - `batch_reauthors_a_false_judgment…` checks that the stored `references` digest equals the reference-set digest.\n- **Failure cases.**\n  - `failed_evidence_write_keeps_the_accepted_judgment_in_usage` forces a write failure by making the evidence path a directory.\n  - `explicit_untracked_reads_abort_without_another_request_or_evidence_write` shows no evidence is written or changed when the run aborts.\n- **Live Bedrock test.** `live_bedrock_authoring` checks `--stdout` output against a real provider and that no `evidence/` directory is created.\n\n**Residual note.** There is no direct single-subject test that asserts `references` in a default-mode file. The batch test covers it through the same `record` function, and the schema is covered by `requirements.ci.schema`'s own tests. I do not consider this a material gap.",
  "references": "c51ee997790716abb90d24ddd3e340402f10480b9cc3cac01f4dc7eed2034142",
